One common way to introduce vulnerabilities in Java web applications is through **Log Injection** (also known as Log Forging). An attacker can inject CRLF (Carriage Return/Line Feed) characters into an input field, which they provide to the application. When these inputs are logged, the attacker can manipulate the log output to forge fake log entries, mask malicious activity, or deceive administrators.

```java
import org.slf4j.Logger;
import org.slf4j.LoggerFactory;

// A controller class handling user login attempts
public class LoginController {
    private static final Logger logger = LoggerFactory.getLogger(LoginController.class);

    public void login(String username) {
        // This implementation is vulnerable to Log Injection.
        // An attacker could use \n characters in the username to inject fake log entries.
        logger.info("Login attempt failed for user: " + username);
    }
}
```

***

```java
import org.slf4j.Logger;
import org.slf4j.LoggerFactory;
import org.owasp.encoder.Encode;

// A secure controller class handling user login attempts
public class LoginController {
    private static final Logger logger = LoggerFactory.getLogger(LoginController.class);

    public void login(String username) {
        // To fix the vulnerability, we sanitize the input before logging.
        // We replace newline characters to prevent CRLF injection and ensure the log integrity.
        String sanitizedUsername = username.replace('\n', '_').replace('\r', '_');

        // Alternatively, using a library like OWASP Encoder is even more robust:
        // String sanitizedUsername = Encode.forJava(username);

        logger.info("Login attempt failed for user: {}", sanitizedUsername);
    }
}
```

